## 4. `include/strata/core/conversation_buffer.hpp` — `operator==` can read past a segment when sizes mismatch mid-walk

**Responsibilities.** `ConversationBuffer` is snapshot-owned segmented host storage; `operator==` compares two buffers byte-for-byte across their segment directories.

**Problem.** The equality loop advances `a`/`b` and `x`/`y` assuming both sides have exactly `size_` bytes of live data. `resize()` guarantees `segments_` sizes sum to `size_`, so the loop is correct *today*, but it has no defensive check: a future mutation bug (a segment whose `size()` exceeds its `capacity()`, or a moved-from buffer compared against a live one) makes `left.size() - x` underflow to `SIZE_MAX` and `memcmp` read out of bounds. A cheap precondition assert plus an early `size_` mismatch return makes the invariant explicit and turns a silent OOB read into a deterministic failure.

**Change.**

```diff
--- a/include/strata/core/conversation_buffer.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/core/conversation_buffer.hpp
@@
     bool operator==(const ConversationBuffer& other) const {
         if (size_ != other.size_) return false;
+        // The walk below consumes exactly size_ bytes from each side; a segment whose live size
+        // exceeds its capacity (a resize bug) would underflow `left.size() - x` and memcmp out of
+        // bounds.  Check the invariant instead of trusting it.
+        size_t live = 0;
+        for (const auto& s : segments_) live += s.size();
+        if (live != size_) return false;
+        size_t other_live = 0;
+        for (const auto& s : other.segments_) other_live += s.size();
+        if (other_live != other.size_) return false;
         size_t a = 0, b = 0, x = 0, y = 0;
```

**Verification.**
1. `conversation_snapshot_test` / `conversation_validation_test` must pass unchanged (valid buffers always satisfy the invariant, so equality results are identical).
2. New unit case in `src/core/conversation_snapshot_test.cpp`: construct two equal buffers, corrupt one by `resize()`ing it down then back up through the public API, and assert equality still reports the true byte difference (and that a hand-corrupted segment size — via a test-only friend hook — returns false rather than crashing).

